---
import Pipeline from '../../components/Pipeline.astro';
import Docs from '../../layouts/Docs.astro';
import { APP_ID, COSIGN_PUB, IMAGES, ORG_ID, REPO } from '../../data/site';

const DESCRIPTION =
  'Each Pulsar image carries an SPDX SBOM, diffed against the build before it. Images, ISOs and the NVIDIA module are signed, and Rekor logs image and ISO signatures.';

const JSONLD = [
  {
    '@type': 'TechArticle',
    headline: 'Pulsar image provenance and signing',
    description: DESCRIPTION,
    about: { '@id': APP_ID },
    author: { '@id': ORG_ID },
    publisher: { '@id': ORG_ID },
    url: new URL('/docs/provenance', Astro.site).href,
  },
];
const SBOM = `oras discover ${IMAGES.vanilla}:latest`;
// The signature is attached in the registry with its Rekor entry, so cosign
// checks the log offline: no --insecure-ignore-tlog.
const VERIFY = `cosign verify --key ${COSIGN_PUB} \\\n  ${IMAGES.vanilla}:latest`;
const TOC = [
  { id: 'one-night', label: 'How an image is built', level: 2 },
  { id: 'verify', label: 'Check it yourself', level: 2 },
  { id: 'keys', label: 'The NVIDIA module key', level: 2 },
  { id: 'by-hand', label: 'Builds started by hand', level: 2 },
];
---

<Docs
  title="Provenance"
  fullTitle="Pulsar image provenance and signing"
  description={DESCRIPTION}
  path="/docs/provenance"
  headings={TOC}
  jsonLd={JSONLD}
>
  <h1>Provenance</h1>
  <p class="intro">
    Each image is built on a machine created for that night’s build and deleted afterwards, and
    ships with its full package list attached. The signing keys are never on that machine. A
    separate signing host holds them.
  </p>

  <h2 id="one-night">How an image is built</h2>
  <Pipeline compact />

  <h2 id="verify">Check it yourself</h2>
  <p>
    Each image has an SPDX SBOM attached in the registry, and <code>oras discover</code> lists it.
    Images and ISOs are signed with Pulsar’s cosign release key, and each signature is logged in
    Rekor, Sigstore’s public transparency log.
  </p>
  <arc-code-block class="theme-fixed-dark" language="bash" filename="sbom" code={SBOM}></arc-code-block>
  <p>
    The build diffs each nightly’s SBOM against the previous one’s. The{' '}
    <a href="/docs/changelog">changelog</a>, <a href="/changelog.json">changelog.json</a> and{' '}
    <code>pulsar changelog</code> all show that diff.
  </p>
  <arc-description-list class="endpoints" layout="horizontal">
    <arc-description-item term="changelog.json"><a href="/changelog.json">the night’s diff, as the build wrote it</a></arc-description-item>
    <arc-description-item term="manifest.json"><a href="/manifest.json">the image’s baked manifest</a></arc-description-item>
    <arc-description-item term="cosign.pub"><a href={`${REPO}/blob/main/keys/cosign.pub`}>the release key that signs images and ISOs</a></arc-description-item>
  </arc-description-list>
  <p>Check an image’s signature and its log entry:</p>
  <arc-code-block class="theme-fixed-dark" language="bash" filename="verify" code={VERIFY}></arc-code-block>
  <p>
    Images and ISOs from before November 9, 2026 were signed with an earlier key, kept as{' '}
    <a href={`${REPO}/blob/main/keys/cosign-before-2026-11-09.pub`}><code>keys/cosign-before-2026-11-09.pub</code></a>.
    Signatures made before Rekor logging began have no log entry and check only with{' '}
    <code>--insecure-ignore-tlog=true</code>. That covers images signed from late September to early
    October 2026, and ISOs from before September 30, 2026.
  </p>
  <p>
    On a Pulsar machine, <code>pulsar verify</code> prints the same check for the exact image you
    booted, by digest, against the key the image ships, and runs it if <code>cosign</code> is
    installed. It only checks. Updates are pulled without a signature policy, so a machine won’t
    refuse an unsigned image on its own.
  </p>
  <p class="hint">
    <a href="/docs/install#iso">Verify an ISO</a> · <a href="/docs/signing">NVIDIA module signing</a>
  </p>

  <h2 id="keys">The NVIDIA module key</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>The NVIDIA variant builds <code>nvidia-open</code> for the image’s exact kernel and signs it.</li>
    <li>
      <code>Containerfile</code> needs no secrets. <code>Containerfile.nvidia</code> sends each
      module to the signing host with a bearer token and attaches the returned signature. Neither
      the key nor the token reaches an image layer.
    </li>
    <li>
      Both images ship the public cert, so it can be enrolled before switching to NVIDIA. The build
      fails if the module’s signer doesn’t match it.
    </li>
    <li>
      If you build your own image, fork it and sign with your own key. Enrolling this cert means
      trusting every module Pulsar signs. The standard image needs no keys.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="by-hand">Builds started by hand</h2>
  <p>
    A build started by hand runs the same pipeline, takes the next version number and is pushed
    under it. It doesn’t move <code>:latest</code>, the SBOM baseline, the published changelog or
    the site, so a machine gets it only if someone switches to that version.
  </p>
  <p class="hint">MIT-licensed. <a href={REPO}>Source</a>.</p>
</Docs>

<style>
  .endpoints {
    display: block;
    margin: var(--space-md) 0;
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--text-sm);
  }
</style>
